In operational amplifier circuit analysis, negative feedback networks are analyzed rapidly by applying the two ideal op-amp assumptions (often called the Golden Rules):

Infinite Open-Loop Gain (A_{ol} \to \infty): When an op-amp operates in its linear region with negative feedback, the voltage difference between the non-inverting and inverting input terminals is driven to zero (a virtual short): \mathbf{V_+ = V_-}
Infinite Input Impedance (R_{in} \to \infty): Because the input stage draws negligible current, the current entering both the non-inverting and inverting terminals is zero: \mathbf{I_+ = I_- = 0\text{ A}}

